{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Wilmslow Show is held at Wilmslow High School, Wilmslow, Cheshire, England, as a one-day event on a Sunday – usually the second Sunday in July.","title":"Wilmslow Show"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Sections include Horticulture, Dogs, Classic Cars, etc.","title":"Wilmslow Show"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Wilmslow High School is a mixed-sex 11–18 comprehensive secondary school in Wilmslow, Cheshire, and a designated Centre of Excellence.","title":"Wilmslow High School"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The school began in 1960 as a grammar school and gradually became a comprehensive school, becoming Wilmslow High School in 1991.","title":"Wilmslow High School"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Dr. James Pullé is the head teacher.","title":"Wilmslow High School"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The school is designated "good" by Ofsted.","title":"Wilmslow High School"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":"<ref name="11/13 Ofsted Report">Ofsted report</ref>","title":"Wilmslow High School"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Conestoga was a literary science fiction and fantasy convention held annually in Tulsa, Oklahoma from 1997 through 2010, after which it was suspended for financial and logistical reasons.","title":"Conestoga (convention)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The convention at first was designated by the year in which it was held.","title":"Conestoga (convention)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" With its sixth incarnation, Conestoga converted to a whole number count, rolling the first five into the number tally, and running through #14 before stopping.","title":"Conestoga (convention)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Celebrating science fiction and fantasy literature and art were staples of the convention, but many who were also interested in horror, anime, and comics attended.","title":"Conestoga (convention)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Popular activities at the convention included filking (filk singing is a play on folk) and gaming.","title":"Conestoga (convention)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Featured programming included a writers track, an art show, a dealer room, a masquerade, and a play put on by the Penguin Playhouse Troupe.","title":"Conestoga (convention)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Dungeness School is a historical schoolhouse building located at 657 Towne Road in Dungeness, Washington.","title":"Dungeness School"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The idea for the school was formed in 1892 by the settlers of the Dungeness area.","title":"Dungeness School"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" On May 10, 1892 a meeting was held in which settlers created a bond for land and a two story school house.","title":"Dungeness School"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The school house opened on February 27, 1893.","title":"Dungeness School"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The school originally had 73 students from the ages of 5 to 20.","title":"Dungeness School"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The school had one teacher who lived on the second floor of the school.","title":"Dungeness School"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Classes were large and books and supplies not easy to get.","title":"Dungeness School"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" School Board minutes of April 8, 1895, show a motion was passed to buy the school a bottle of ink.","title":"Dungeness School"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Hawthorn Hall is a former country house in Hall Road, Wilmslow, Cheshire, England.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It originated in about 1610 as a timber-framed yeoman house for John Chavman of mnc.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was improved and encased in brick for John Leigh in 1698.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Its use changed in the 19th century, and in 1835 it opened as a homeless shelter school.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The building has since been used as offices.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It is constructed in plum-coloured brick, with a Kerridge stone-slate roof, a stone ridge, and three brick chimneys.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Parts of the timber-framing can still be seen in the roof gables, and in an internal wall.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The plan consists of a long rectangle.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" The house is in 2½ storeys, and has a near-symmetrical north front.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" There are four gables with bargeboards and mace finials. Each gable contains a pair of wooden mullioned and transomed windows.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" In the centre is a doorway, flanked by plain pilasters, and surmounted by a segmental hood framing a cartouche containing the date 1698.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":11,"text":" At the top of the hall, above the door, is a small balustrade, behind which is a half-glazed lantern with a cupola and a weathervane.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":12,"text":" The south front is similar to the north front, although the door is not central.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":13,"text":" This door is flanked by fluted pilasters, and surmounted by a plaque with a lion rampant.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":14,"text":" The east front has two gables.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":15,"text":" The architectural historian Nikolaus Pevsner comments that the house is "good to look at, though conservative for its date".","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":16,"text":" The house, together with parts of the garden walls, is recorded in the National Heritage List for England as a designated Grade II* listed building.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The California State Summer School for the Arts, commonly known as CSSSA ("SEE-SUH"), is a rigorous four-week, pre-professional visual and performing arts training program for high school students held each summer at the California Institute of the Arts.","title":"California State Summer School for the Arts"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The goal of CSSSA is to provide a supportive environment in which students acquire experience and training that extends beyond the practice and improvement of aesthetics and technique.","title":"California State Summer School for the Arts"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Artistic disciplines offered by the program include: animation, creative writing, dance, film and video, music (including vocal arts), theatre arts, and visual arts.","title":"California State Summer School for the Arts"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Its purpose is to provide a training ground for future artists who wish to pursue careers in the arts and entertainment industries in California.","title":"California State Summer School for the Arts"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Admitted students are designated "California Arts Scholars", honored throughout the state’s fifty-eight counties.","title":"California State Summer School for the Arts"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Upon completion of the program, the students are awarded a Governor's Medallion, the highest distinction in California for artistically talented students.","title":"California State Summer School for the Arts"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Founded in 1987, CSSSA is a California State agency funded through a unique public/private partnership.","title":"California State Summer School for the Arts"}],"peer_messages":["text":" It originated in about 1610 as a timber-framed yeoman house for John Chavman of mnc.","title":"Hawthorn Hall"}
